{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794817252,"nonce":"HB9PWz1WI5S5KDahbU1G56DEI50=","message":"Drivers repinned to 10afe2f with Ash v0.1.19: hxn-gpu-webgpu b04c571, hxn-gpu-webgl2 8a790f3. Both add hxcpp threaded jobs to their browser CI (macOS GPU, Linux SwiftShader). Locally on macOS Chrome, all three checks pass on both drivers: cpp --wasm, cpp --wasm --threads, hl --wasm --threads.\n\nawait on a GPU future still hangs in a threaded hxcpp build. A probe that awaited requestAdapter() (WebGPU) and completed() (WebGL2) on main's pthread never returned. WebGPU's promises and the WebGL2 fence poll both settle on the event loop of the thread holding the device or context, and await's sleep loop on that pthread never yields to it. Futures settled by the library's own threads (examples/downloads) do work with await. The drivers' docs say to use then under hxcpp, with or without threads. Making await work there would mean running the GPU on another thread (an OffscreenCanvas worker of its own, as Ash's browser agent does), or yielding to the event loop inside await.","files":null}]}